  - Both P1 and P2 are heterozygotes
  - Lane D has 1,2,3, but not 4

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 3](assets/lect05b-unknown_genotype_problems/image_003.png)

@right

- (a) 1,3 = gene; 2,4 = gene
- (b) Lane D has 1,2,3, but not 4
  - D is heterozygote for gene 1,3
    - probability of ½ for heteroz. from heteroz. parents
  - D is homozygote for gene 2,4
    - probability of ¼ for homoz. from heteroz. parents

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 3](assets/lect05b-unknown_genotype_problems/image_003.png)

@right

- (a) 1,3 = gene; 2,4 = gene
- (b) genes are independent
  - hetero ½ × homo ¼ = [1/8]{color=red}

=== layout: section

# [Unknown Alleles]{color=red} Hartl Question 3.23

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 4](assets/lect05b-unknown_genotype_problems/image_004.png)

@right

- Based on their coat colors of the parent alone, we can predict that genotypes of\:
- Female = solid tan = aa S&ndash;
- Male = solid black = A&ndash; S&ndash;

- [dashes are used to say that we don&rsquo;t know the second allele]{color=red}
- [&lsquo;A&ndash;&lsquo; means &lsquo;AA&rsquo; or &lsquo;Aa&rsquo;]{color=red}

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 4](assets/lect05b-unknown_genotype_problems/image_004.png)

@right

- Look at genotypes of offspring\:
  - 2 solid tan\: S&ndash;aa
  - 2 solid black\: S&ndash;A&ndash;
  - 1 spotted tan\: ssaa
  - 1 spotted black\: ssA&ndash;

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 4](assets/lect05b-unknown_genotype_problems/image_004.png)

@right

- Female = solid tan = aa S&ndash;
- Male = solid black = A&ndash; S&ndash;
- Because of tan offspring (aa) is produced, the male must also have one recessive allele for gene A.
- Female = aa S&ndash;
- Male = Aa S&ndash;
- Since there are recessive spotted offspring (ss), both parents must have one recessive allele for gene S.
- [Female = aa Ss]{color=red}
- [Male = Aa Ss]{color=red}

